@@ PE1 | Predictive
An NGO is planning a rural water project. The donors define success as the number of wells built, the local government as compliance with district plans, and community leaders as reliable water access for every household. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a workshop to agree shared objectives and success measures
B) Use the donors' definition, since the donors are funding the entire water project
C) Record all three definitions separately and report against each one independently
D) Ask the local government to decide, since the project must follow district plans
= A
? A common vision requires agreed objectives and success measures. Choosing one group's view or reporting against conflicting definitions leaves stakeholders misaligned and invites conflict later.

@@ PE1 | Agile
During a sprint review, several developers admit they cannot explain how their stories contribute to the product goal. What should the scrum master do?
A) Ask the product owner to restate the product goal and link each sprint goal to it
B) Tell the developers that understanding the product goal is the product owner's job only
C) Add a slide about the company's mission statement to the start of every sprint review
D) Ask the sponsor to explain the business case to the developers at the next all-hands meeting
= A
? The product goal gives the team direction; linking sprint goals to it keeps purpose visible every sprint. A generic mission slide or a one-off presentation does not connect daily work to the goal.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Three subcontractor crews join a telecom network rollout in its second phase. They will work alongside the internal engineering team. What should the project manager do first?
A) Hold an onboarding session on objectives, priorities and working agreements
B) Send the crews the full project management plan and ask them to read it before starting
C) Let each crew follow its own company's practices, since they are external contractors
D) Ask the internal engineers to supervise the crews closely until they understand the goals
= A
? New team members need a shared understanding of objectives and agreements. A document alone, separate practices or close supervision do not create a common vision.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A product vision statement reads: "Be the best fitness app." The team finds it hard to use for decisions. What should the product owner do?
A) Refine it to state the target users, their need and the key differentiating benefit
B) Keep the statement, since a short and ambitious vision motivates the team to work harder
C) Replace the vision with a detailed list of all the features planned for the next two years
D) Ask the developers to write their own visions and use whichever version receives most votes
= A
? A useful vision identifies who the product is for, what need it meets and why it is different (for example, using the elevator-pitch format). A feature list is a backlog, not a vision.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
At the kickoff meeting for a 40-store retail refit, what should the project manager emphasise to build a common understanding?
A) Objectives, success criteria, roles, and how decisions will be made
B) The detailed technical specifications of every fixture to be installed in each store
C) The penalties that will apply to team members who miss their assigned deadlines
D) The project manager's personal approach and preferences for managing the project
= A
? Kickoffs align everyone on why, what success looks like, who does what and how decisions are made. Technical detail, threats or personal preferences do not build shared direction.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A shift in the market means the product strategy must pivot from small businesses to large enterprises. The team has been building for small businesses for six months. What should the project manager do?
A) Update the vision with the sponsor and product owner and explain the reasons
B) Keep the team focused on the current backlog until the new strategy has been fully detailed
C) Announce the new target market by email and ask the team to adjust their work accordingly
D) Ask the team to continue building both versions of the product until the market becomes clear
= A
? When direction changes, the vision must be updated by those accountable and communicated with the reasons, so the team understands and commits to the new direction.

@@ PE1 | Agile | multi
Which two artifacts best establish a shared understanding at the start of an agile project? (Choose two.)
A) A product vision statement
B) A team charter or working agreements
C) A detailed Gantt chart for every feature
D) A procurement statement of work
E) A risk audit report
= A, B
? The vision clarifies why and for whom; the team charter clarifies how the team will work together. A detailed Gantt chart contradicts adaptive planning, and the other documents serve different purposes.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
In a factory, the production manager refuses to stop the line for the equipment installation planned next week, because orders are high. The project engineer insists the stoppage is essential. What should the project manager do?
A) Bring both together to find an installation window that suits both needs
B) Ask the sponsor to order the production manager to stop the line next week as planned
C) Postpone the installation indefinitely until order volumes fall to a more normal level
D) Let the project engineer decide, since the installation is the project's responsibility
= A
? Collaborative problem-solving can uncover options such as weekend or phased installation. Forcing via the sponsor, indefinite delay or one-sided decisions damage relationships or the project.

@@ PE2 | Agile
During sprint planning, the product owner pushes the developers to take on 20% more work than their recent velocity. The developers are frustrated. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate, reminding everyone that developers decide how much they can take on
B) Support the product owner, since the product owner is accountable for maximising value
C) Split the difference so that the team accepts 10% more work than its recent velocity
D) Cancel the planning session and ask the project sponsor to settle the disagreement
= A
? In Scrum, the product owner decides what is most valuable and the developers decide how much they can deliver. The scrum master coaches both on these accountabilities.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Two vendors blame each other for a failed integration between their systems. Each says the other's interface does not follow the specification. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the integration logs against the interface specification together with both vendors
B) Withhold payment from both vendors until one of them accepts responsibility for the failure
C) Ask the internal team to fix the integration, since the vendors cannot agree with each other
D) Ask the procurement department to choose which vendor is responsible based on the contracts
= A
? Focusing on facts — logs and the agreed specification — moves the discussion from blame to solving the problem. Contract terms then determine responsibilities for any corrective work.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two team members are arguing loudly about a minor issue. Emotions are running high, and neither is listening. Which approach is most appropriate at this moment?
A) Withdraw temporarily to allow a short cooling-off period first
B) Force a decision immediately so that the argument stops in front of the team
C) Compromise by asking each of them to give up half of what they want
D) Smooth the issue by telling them their disagreement is not important
= A
? When emotions are high, a short, temporary withdrawal can allow people to calm down. The issue must then be addressed — withdrawal is never a permanent solution.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A senior developer refuses to pair-program with junior colleagues, although the team agreed to try pairing for knowledge sharing. Tension is growing. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Talk privately with the senior developer to understand the concerns about pairing
B) Remind the senior developer in the next daily scrum that the team agreed to pair
C) Ask the senior developer's manager to require participation in pair programming
D) Drop the pairing experiment, since the most experienced developer does not support it
= A
? Understanding the person's concerns privately is the first step; they may suggest a better way to share knowledge. Public pressure or management mandates rarely build commitment.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
A team member reports that a colleague has repeatedly made offensive remarks about her ethnicity. What should the project manager do?
A) Follow the organisation's policy and involve human resources promptly
B) Facilitate a meeting between the two so that they can resolve it together
C) Ask her to ignore the remarks until the project has been completed
D) Move her to another team to separate the two people immediately
= A
? Harassment is not an ordinary conflict to be negotiated; organisational policy and HR procedures must be followed promptly to protect the person and the organisation.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Team members in Asia complain that all meetings are held at times convenient only for the European office, while European members say late meetings are impossible for them. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a team agreement such as rotating meeting times
B) Keep the current schedule, since most of the stakeholders are based in Europe
C) Ask the Asian team members to join from home late at night when needed
D) Split the team into two separate teams that never need to meet together
= A
? A collaborative solution that shares the inconvenience fairly and supports asynchronous participation respects everyone. One-sided arrangements damage inclusion and engagement.

@@ PE2 | Agile
In planning poker, testers consistently estimate stories much higher than developers, causing friction. What should the team do?
A) Have the testers and developers explain their assumptions and re-estimate together
B) Use only the developers' estimates, since developers do most of the work on stories
C) Average all estimates automatically so that the disagreement disappears from discussion
D) Let the scrum master choose the final estimate whenever the team disagrees on a story
= A
? Divergent estimates often reveal hidden work, such as testing effort. Discussing assumptions improves estimates and shared understanding.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A capable developer asks the project manager for permission before making even small technical decisions. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify decision boundaries and encourage decisions within them
B) Continue approving each decision, since the developer is asking for guidance
C) Ask the product owner to approve the developer's technical decisions instead
D) Assign a senior developer to approve all of the developer's decisions from now on
= A
? Empowerment requires clear boundaries and encouragement. Continuing to approve every decision creates dependency and slows the team.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A team member asks to attend a two-week certification course during the most critical month of the project. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree a timing for the course that supports both the person's development and the project
B) Refuse the request, since the project's needs always come before personal development
C) Approve the request immediately, since training is always good for the organisation
D) Ask the functional manager to decide without giving any information about the schedule
= A
? Supporting development while protecting the project requires a conversation about timing. A flat refusal demotivates; automatic approval ignores the project's needs.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
In design meetings, one experienced engineer dominates the discussion, and others have stopped contributing. What should the project manager do?
A) Use facilitation that invites all voices and talk to the engineer privately
B) Let the engineer continue, since the most experienced person usually has the best ideas
C) Exclude the engineer from future design meetings so that others can speak freely
D) Tell the engineer in front of the group to stop dominating the design discussions
= A
? Inclusive facilitation (round-robin, silent brainstorming) and a private conversation balance participation without humiliating anyone.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team wants to try mob programming for complex stories, but a line manager is sceptical about "five people working on one computer". What should the project manager do?
A) Support a timeboxed experiment with agreed measures, reviewed in the retrospective
B) Refuse the experiment, since the line manager's concerns about productivity are valid
C) Approve mob programming permanently for all work, since the team asked for it
D) Ask the line manager to decide which development practices the team may use
= A
? Empirical experimentation with clear measures respects the team's autonomy and gives the line manager evidence. Teams own their way of working within organisational boundaries.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Team members complain about cramped office space and below-market salaries. The project manager is considering a monthly award ceremony to raise motivation. According to Herzberg, what is the most likely outcome?
A) Awards will have limited effect until the hygiene issues, such as space and pay, are addressed
B) Awards will fully resolve the problem, since recognition is a strong motivator for everyone
C) Office space and salary are motivators, so improving them will maximise satisfaction
D) Neither awards nor working conditions affect motivation according to Herzberg's theory
= A
? Hygiene factors such as working conditions and pay cause dissatisfaction when inadequate; motivators like recognition work best once hygiene issues are addressed.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A recently formed team argues frequently about coding standards and tools, and velocity is unstable. Which stage of team development is the team most likely in, and what should the leader do?
A) Storming; coach the team and facilitate agreements on standards
B) Forming; make all decisions for the team until it stabilises
C) Performing; delegate fully and stay out of the discussions
D) Adjourning; prepare to release team members to other projects
= A
? Frequent disagreements about ways of working indicate storming. The leader coaches and facilitates working agreements to help the team move to norming.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
After the organisation introduces AI coding assistants, some team members worry that their jobs are at risk and are reluctant to use the tools. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the concerns openly and involve the team in how AI supports their work
B) Make the use of AI assistants mandatory and track each person's usage statistics weekly
C) Tell the team that the tools are optional and avoid discussing their concerns further
D) Reassure the team that no jobs will ever change, without checking with management
= A
? Addressing fears openly and involving people in shaping how AI augments their work builds trust and adoption. Mandates or false reassurances undermine trust.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A contractor embedded in the project team is consistently late with deliverables. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the performance with the contractor and raise it with the vendor's management per the contract
B) Issue a formal written warning to the contractor in the same way as for an employee of the organisation
C) Reduce the contractor's hours immediately without informing the vendor or reviewing the contract
D) Ignore the issue, since contractors are managed by their own company and not by the project
= A
? Performance issues with contractors are discussed with them and handled through the contractual relationship with the vendor, not through internal HR processes.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A junior developer who started enthusiastically is now struggling with a complex codebase and losing confidence. According to situational leadership, which style fits best?
A) Coaching — high direction and high support
B) Delegating — low direction and low support
C) Directing — high direction and low support
D) Supporting — low direction and high support
= A
? A "disillusioned learner" with some competence and low commitment benefits from coaching: continued direction plus encouragement and support.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A factory automation project will change several jobs on the production line. Union representatives have asked for information but have not yet been involved. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the union representatives early and involve them in planning the changes to the jobs
B) Wait until the equipment is installed before involving the union in any discussions about jobs
C) Ask human resources to manage all communication with the union on the project's behalf
D) Share information with the union only when the law requires the organisation to do so
= A
? Unions are influential stakeholders with legitimate interests in job changes. Early engagement reduces resistance and may improve the solution.

@@ PE4 | Agile
Stakeholder analysis shows that the app's end users have high interest but low power over the project. What is the most appropriate engagement approach?
A) Keep them informed, for example through demos and feedback channels
B) Manage them closely, involving them in every decision about the product
C) Keep them satisfied with occasional executive briefings
D) Monitor them with minimal effort, since they have little power
= A
? High-interest, low-power stakeholders are kept informed. In product development their feedback is also valuable, so demos and feedback channels are especially useful.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The organisation announces a restructuring that changes several department heads involved in the project. What should the project manager do?
A) Re-identify stakeholders and update the register and engagement plan
B) Continue with the current engagement plan until the restructuring is complete
C) Ask the sponsor to manage all of the new department heads personally
D) Postpone stakeholder engagement until the next phase gate review
= A
? Stakeholder identification is continuous; organisational changes require updating the register and engagement plan.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Community leaders in the villages where a health programme will operate have not attended any project meetings held in the capital. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage them locally, in their language and through respected intermediaries
B) Continue holding meetings in the capital and send them the meeting minutes
C) Proceed without their involvement, since the ministry has approved the project
D) Ask the donors to require the community leaders' attendance at the meetings
= A
? Culturally appropriate, accessible engagement is essential for stakeholders whose support determines success. Distant meetings and top-down mandates ignore their context.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The product owner is overwhelmed by requests from many stakeholders, who now feel ignored because their items never appear in sprints. What should the project manager suggest?
A) Regular, transparent backlog review sessions where stakeholders discuss the priorities
B) Adding every stakeholder request to the next sprint so that nobody feels ignored
C) Asking stakeholders to stop sending requests until the current release is finished
D) Letting each stakeholder prioritise their own items directly in the product backlog
= A
? Transparency about prioritisation, with regular opportunities to discuss trade-offs, maintains engagement while the product owner keeps accountability for ordering the backlog.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Which tool would best show the project manager the gap between stakeholders' current and desired levels of engagement?
A) Stakeholder engagement assessment matrix
B) Resource breakdown structure
C) Responsibility assignment matrix
D) Probability and impact matrix
= A
? The engagement assessment matrix records current (C) and desired (D) engagement for each stakeholder, highlighting gaps that need action.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
A key business stakeholder who strongly supports the project will leave the company next month. The replacement has not yet been named. What should the project manager do?
A) Plan the handover: capture their knowledge and brief the replacement
B) Wait until the replacement starts and then repeat the stakeholder analysis from scratch
C) Ask the departing stakeholder to approve all pending decisions before leaving the company
D) Remove the role from the stakeholder register, since its future holder is unknown
= A
? Proactive handover preserves knowledge and support. Rushing decisions or deleting the role from the register creates risk.

@@ PE4 | Agile | multi
The sponsor has stopped attending reviews and rarely responds to emails. Which two actions are most likely to re-engage the sponsor? (Choose two.)
A) Short, focused updates linked to the business outcomes the sponsor cares about
B) Inviting the sponsor to key reviews where a specific decision is needed
C) Sending the sponsor the complete sprint backlogs every week
D) Reducing contact with the sponsor to avoid being a nuisance
E) Asking the PMO to complain to the sponsor's manager
= A, B
? Sponsors engage when communication is relevant to their goals and their input is needed. Detailed data dumps, withdrawal or complaints do not rebuild engagement.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The customer expects the first prototype of a medical device to be completely free of defects. The prototype is meant for early usability testing. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the prototype's purpose and agree suitable acceptance criteria
B) Delay the prototype until it can be delivered completely free of any defects
C) Accept the expectation and add extra testing to the prototype phase
D) Tell the customer that defects in prototypes are not the project's concern
= A
? Aligning expectations means agreeing what each deliverable is for and how it will be judged. Treating a usability prototype like a final product wastes time and money.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Senior stakeholders expect a fixed scope, a fixed date and a fixed budget for a new product being built with Scrum. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain adaptive planning and agree which elements are fixed and how scope will flex
B) Accept all three constraints and ask the team to commit to the full scope
C) Switch to a predictive approach so that the stakeholders' expectations are met
D) Avoid the discussion and let the stakeholders discover the trade-offs over time
= A
? In adaptive delivery, time and cost are often fixed while scope flexes by priority. Explicitly agreeing this aligns expectations early.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
A sales manager promised a client a new feature by the end of the month without consulting the product team, which cannot deliver it before next quarter. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree a realistic date with sales and the product owner, then inform the client together
B) Ask the team to work overtime so that the sales manager's promise to the client can be kept
C) Inform the client directly that the sales manager's promise was a mistake and cannot be kept
D) Ask the sponsor to discipline the sales manager for making promises without approval
= A
? Aligning internal stakeholders first, then communicating consistently to the client, protects the relationship and the team. Overtime and public blame are poor responses.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The finance department and the operations department each expect the new reports in a different format and level of detail. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify each department's needs and agree on the formats with both before design continues
B) Design the reports according to the finance department's needs, since finance funds the project
C) Build two complete versions of every report so that each department gets what it wants
D) Ask the developers to choose the format that is easiest to implement for both departments
= A
? Clarifying needs and agreeing formats early prevents rework and disappointment. Defaulting to one group or doubling the work are poor choices.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Management expects the team's velocity to double after adding four developers to a team of five. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that onboarding and coordination limit early gains, and forecast realistically
B) Confirm the expectation, since doubling the team should double the velocity within a sprint
C) Ask the new developers to start immediately on separate features to maximise output
D) Report velocity per developer so that management can see each person's contribution
= A
? Adding people increases communication paths and onboarding effort; velocity may even dip initially. Setting realistic expectations with data maintains trust.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The product team plans to submit a device for certification in three months, but the regulator's typical review time suggests six months. Expectations differ widely. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the regulator early to agree the evidence needed and realistic review milestones
B) Submit in three months and hope the regulator reviews the application faster than usual
C) Keep the three-month target internally, since a shorter target motivates the team
D) Wait for the regulator's feedback after submission before adjusting the plan
= A
? Early engagement with the regulator aligns expectations on evidence and timing and reduces the risk of late surprises.

@@ PE5 | Predictive | multi
Which two practices best align stakeholder expectations at the start of a predictive project? (Choose two.)
A) Interviewing key stakeholders about their success criteria
B) Documenting deliverables, acceptance criteria and exclusions in the scope statement
C) Postponing scope discussions until the first deliverable is ready
D) Assuming stakeholders share the sponsor's expectations
E) Letting each stakeholder define success independently
= A, B
? Understanding success criteria and documenting what is and is not included set clear, shared expectations. Postponing or assuming creates surprises later.

@@ PE6 | Agile
After a release with performance problems, customer satisfaction scores dropped sharply. What should the project manager and product owner do?
A) Communicate a plan to fix the performance issues and prioritise them in the backlog
B) Wait for the next release, since satisfaction usually recovers when features are added
C) Stop collecting satisfaction scores until the performance issues have been resolved
D) Tell customers that performance problems are normal in early versions of products
= A
? Managing expectations after a setback means acknowledging the problem, showing a plan and following through visibly.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A department head is angry because a design change affecting her team was approved without her being informed. What should the project manager do?
A) Acknowledge the gap, explain the change and involve her in future
B) Explain that the change control board approved it, so no further discussion is needed now
C) Ask the sponsor to explain the change to her, since the sponsor chairs the change board
D) Reverse the change to restore the department head's trust in the project team
= A
? Owning the communication gap and fixing the process restores trust. Hiding behind the decision or reversing an approved change are inappropriate.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
A stakeholder demands weekly face-to-face meetings with the project manager to discuss progress. The project manager's calendar is already full. What should the project manager do?
A) Understand the need behind the request and agree an efficient alternative, such as a short call
B) Accept the weekly meetings, since the stakeholder's satisfaction is more important than other tasks
C) Decline the request, since all the information the stakeholder needs is in the monthly report
D) Delegate the meetings to a junior team member without informing the stakeholder in advance
= A
? Exploring the underlying need often reveals a more efficient way to meet it. Simply agreeing or refusing ignores either the PM's capacity or the stakeholder's needs.

@@ PE6 | Agile
Several stakeholders are frustrated that their backlog items have not been started after three months. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the priorities and involve stakeholders in future prioritisation
B) Move the stakeholders' items to the top of the backlog to reduce their frustration
C) Remove the stakeholders' items from the backlog, since they are unlikely to be built
D) Ask the team to work on the stakeholders' items secretly in their spare time
= A
? Transparency about priorities and involvement in prioritisation manage expectations without abandoning value-based ordering.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The sponsor approved a revised schedule baseline after a major scope change. What should the project manager do next regarding stakeholders?
A) Communicate the new baseline and the reasons for it to all affected stakeholders
B) Update the documents quietly and let stakeholders notice the new dates themselves
C) Inform only the project team, since external stakeholders do not need to know
D) Wait until the next quarterly report to mention the revised schedule
= A
? Stakeholders should be informed promptly of approved baseline changes so their expectations and plans align with the new commitments.

@@ PE6 | Agile
A customer asks to see a demonstration every day instead of at the end of each two-week sprint. What is the best response?
A) Offer a staging environment for ongoing feedback, plus sprint reviews
B) Agree to daily demonstrations, since customer collaboration is an agile value
C) Refuse, since Scrum allows customers to see the product only at sprint reviews
D) Ask the customer to send all feedback in writing at the end of the release
= A
? A staging environment gives the customer continuous visibility without disrupting the team with daily demos; sprint reviews remain the formal feedback event.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
After final acceptance, the customer asks for several additional features "for free, given our good relationship". What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that the requests are outside the agreed scope and can be handled as new work
B) Deliver the features at no cost to preserve the relationship for future projects
C) Ignore the requests, since the project has already been formally accepted
D) Ask the team to build the features in their spare time without charging the customer
= A
? Managing expectations means being clear and respectful about scope boundaries. New requests become a change or new contract, not unpaid work.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Which practice best reduces a team's dependence on a few experts for production support?
A) Rotating support duty among team members with pairing on difficult incidents
B) Assigning all support work to the experts, since they resolve incidents fastest
C) Asking the experts to write a support manual in their spare time
D) Hiring an external support company to handle all incidents
= A
? Rotation with pairing spreads tacit knowledge across the team, reducing single points of failure.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
New packaging machines will be maintained by the plant's maintenance staff after the project. How should the project manager ensure effective knowledge transfer?
A) Combine manuals with hands-on training during commissioning, led by the vendor's engineers
B) Provide the vendor's manuals at handover and ask the maintenance staff to read them
C) Ask the maintenance staff to learn by repairing the machines when they first break down
D) Keep the vendor's engineers on site permanently to maintain the machines for the plant
= A
? Hands-on training during commissioning transfers practical, tacit knowledge, supported by documentation for reference.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
The team rarely uses the lessons learned register, although it contains many entries. What should the project manager do?
A) Make lessons easy to find and review relevant ones briefly at planning sessions
B) Require each team member to read the whole register once per month
C) Delete old entries so that the register becomes shorter and easier to read
D) Stop maintaining the register, since the team does not use it
= A
? Knowledge is used when it is accessible and relevant at the moment of need, for example in planning sessions.

@@ PE7 | Agile
A new developer joins an established Scrum team mid-release. What is the most effective way to bring the developer up to speed?
A) Pair the developer with experienced team members on real backlog items
B) Give the developer two weeks to read all of the documentation alone
C) Ask the developer to observe the team for a full sprint before contributing
D) Assign the developer only simple bug fixes until the release is complete
= A
? Pairing on real work transfers knowledge quickly and builds relationships, while the new member contributes value early.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
The project manager will leave the company in three weeks, halfway through the project. A successor has been named. What should the outgoing project manager do?
A) Prepare a structured handover and introduce the successor to stakeholders
B) Send the successor access to the project folders on the last day
C) Ask the team to brief the successor after the project manager has left
D) Delay the departure until the project has been completed
= A
? A structured handover covering status, risks, issues, decisions and relationships — with personal introductions — preserves continuity and knowledge.

@@ PE7 | Agile | multi
Which two are examples of tacit knowledge? (Choose two.)
A) A senior engineer's instinct about which customers need extra attention
B) An experienced developer's judgment about where defects usually hide
C) The user manual for the billing system
D) The documented deployment procedure
E) The contract with the hosting provider
= A, B
? Tacit knowledge is personal and experience-based, hard to write down. Manuals, procedures and contracts are explicit knowledge.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
A project team grows from 7 to 11 people, including the project manager. How many additional communication channels are created?
A) 34
B) 4
C) 21
D) 55
= A
? With 7 people there are 7 × 6 / 2 = 21 channels; with 11 there are 11 × 10 / 2 = 55. The increase is 34.

@@ PE8 | Agile
The daily scrum has turned into a status report in which each developer reports to the project manager. What should the scrum master do?
A) Coach the developers to focus on progress toward the sprint goal and talk to each other
B) Ask the project manager to run the daily scrum more strictly and shorten each update
C) Replace the daily scrum with a written status report sent to the project manager
D) Allow the practice to continue, since the project manager needs the information
= A
? The daily scrum is for developers to coordinate toward the sprint goal. Coaching restores its purpose; the PM can get information from transparent artifacts.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
Stakeholders in three countries have received inconsistent messages about the project's go-live date from different team members. What should the project manager do?
A) Set a single source of information and agree who issues official updates
B) Ask all team members to stop communicating with stakeholders in other countries
C) Send each country a separate update written by its local team members
D) Wait until the go-live date is final before communicating anything further
= A
? A single source of truth and clear communication roles, defined in the communications plan, prevent inconsistent messages.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
An accident occurs on the construction site, and a worker is injured. After ensuring that the worker receives medical care, what should the project manager do regarding communication?
A) Follow the crisis communication plan and inform authorities and stakeholders
B) Wait until the internal investigation is complete before informing anyone outside the team
C) Ask the team not to discuss the accident with anyone until the project is completed
D) Post a detailed description of the accident on social media to show transparency
= A
? Safety incidents require prompt, accurate communication to authorities and stakeholders according to the crisis plan and legal requirements.

@@ PE8 | Agile
The team needs to record important technical design decisions so that future developers understand why they were made. Which approach is most suitable?
A) Short decision records stored in the team's shared repository
B) Detailed minutes of every meeting sent by email to the team
C) Verbal explanations from the architect when someone asks
D) A formal report to the steering committee for each decision
= A
? Lightweight decision records in a shared repository (pull communication) preserve rationale efficiently. Email minutes get lost, and verbal explanations depend on one person.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
Two hundred stakeholders with very different levels of interest need access to a detailed monthly progress report. What is the most efficient approach?
A) Publish the report on a portal and send a short summary notification
B) Email the full report to all two hundred stakeholders every month
C) Present the full report in a monthly meeting with all stakeholders
D) Send the report only to stakeholders who ask for it by phone
= A
? Pull communication suits large audiences and detailed content, while a short push notification ensures people know it is available.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
After explaining a complex interface change to a vendor by phone, the project manager is unsure the vendor understood it. What should the project manager do?
A) Ask the vendor to summarise the change and confirm it in writing
B) Assume the vendor understood, since the vendor did not ask any questions
C) Repeat the explanation more slowly in a second phone call the next day
D) Send the full specification and ask the vendor to read it again
= A
? Feedback and written confirmation check understanding and create a shared record — the receiver paraphrasing is a key active-listening technique.
